  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Sticker You Were Missing",
    "overview": "Every topic mastered is a numbered slot filled on an area's page. Progress is a page filling up, not a percentage and not a winding lesson path. The screen is a full field of the area's colour, white sheets for work, near-black green ink, and one yellow button that goes and gets the next sticker.\n\nDensity is low and the phone comes first: one thing to do, large names and numbers, thumb-sized targets. The register is young and credible, for students of 15 and 16 in short sessions and mixed light.\n\n**Review status.** This section records what the code ships. The student surface has not yet been reviewed rendered against live data, so empty, long-label and full-album states are unconfirmed on screen, and values here may move after that review. The 33 subtopic sticker pictures are simple provisional SVG glyphs.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Full fields of per-area colour with white type.",
      "Numbered slots, on a four-column page on the phone and six on desktop.",
      "Yellow means the thing to go and get.",
      "Anton for names and numbers, Figtree for everything read.",
      "A physical press on the primary button, and one authored landing."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Field Rule",
        "body": "An area owns the whole field in its colour, and every word on a field is white. A new area colour must hold 4.5:1 against white.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Earned Colour Rule",
        "body": "A filled sticker means earned, and nothing else fills a slot. An empty slot shows only its number, on a darker shade of the field.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Names and Numbers Rule",
        "body": "Anton sets names, numbers and the words on the main button, always at 400. Anything read as a sentence is Figtree.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Press Edge Rule",
        "body": "The solid edge belongs to the primary action, in a darker shade of that button's own colour. It is a key you push, chosen with the world, never a decorative offset.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Next One Rule",
        "body": "Only the next sticker lifts and tilts (-4deg). The student's own portrait may tilt (-3deg). Nothing else leans.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Landing Rule",
        "body": "The sticker dropping into its slot (spring, 0.7s, after 0.8s) is the only celebration, and it plays once on a fresh close. Under reduced motion it fades, and the XP count-up shows its number at once.",
        "section": "components"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
